  "prompt_text": "This figure is a conceptual cross-section of an ice sheet showing how surface meltwater and subglacial lakes interact from the snowy interior toward lower, icier areas. Ice flows from left to right. Upstream, a buried or stable lake beneath the ice is associated with slight surface flattening. Farther down, firn and ice zones are separated by the ELA, and small surface melt ponds feed a larger lake; some of this meltwater is routed vertically to the bed.\n\nBeneath the ice, the diagram emphasizes a connected hydrologic system in sediments above hard bedrock. Water reaches subglacial lakes from basal water inputs, frictional heating, and geothermal heat. One central “active lake” appears to fill and drain, producing local elevation changes at the surface: a depression over one lake and uplift over another area. Drainage then propagates through linked water bodies as “cascading drainage,” moving through saturated and soft sediments toward the margin.\n\nOverall, the image shows that lake activity under ice is dynamic rather than isolated: surface melt, internal heating, and sedimentary pathways combine to control filling, drainage, ice-flow response, and potentially a downstream outburst flood or submarine discharge.",
